  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nCurrently, troughing is centered to our northwest with high pressure \nbuilding in behind the recent frontal boundary. This has and will \ncontinue to promote brisk northerly to northeasterly winds into the \nevening hours. Tonight, the surface high will settle just to our \nnortheast, leading to a decrease in winds and strong radiational \ncooling conditions. Frost development is expected across the \nCumberland Plateau, southwest Virginia, and in and near the \nTennessee and north Carolina mountains. On Sunday, troughing will \nmerge with a system in the Atlantic. Height rises and light \nnortheasterly flow are expected, leading to the start of a warming \ntrend and highs back into the 60s for most. Radiational cooling will \nallow for another cool night and patchy fog Sunday night into Monday \nmorning, mainly in northeastern areas.\n\nThe pattern for Tuesday through Thursday will remain largely \nunchanged with ridging to the west and the Atlantic system to our \neast. Height rises will allow for daytime highs to get back into the \n70s with cool overnight lows continuing due to subsidence from \ncontinued high pressure. Late in the week, further height rises will \npush daytime highs further into the 70s, but no rain is expected.",
